My first really good game in 3.1 was going well.  All of the important
intrinsics, Vorpal Blade, great armor (GDSM, GoD, SoR, HoB, BoS, CoD, all
enchanted, rustproofed, and blessed), knew spells up to J and had
the critical books stashed in my blessed bag of holding, holy water, blank
scrolls, magic markers, lots of food, gems almost all identified and 
stashed, all of the useful rings and amulets, a spare wand of wishing, 
and lots of other useful wands.  I had long since genocided 
N, R, n, and L.  The problem with all of those goodies is that they 
weighed a ton and even with maxed out stats and my BoH I was juggling
things in and out to keep from slowing down.  I had just picked up a 
wand of cancellation to see if it would stop the Wizard from summoning
pets and casting other pesky spells.  This was a *bad* move, considering
how I was moving stuff wholesale into my bag.

After reading about the problems with accension in 3.1 (e.g. you need all
sorts of specific goodies to survive the endgame), I was a bit agravated
when my wizardess exploded her bag of holding while standing on the altar
on level 51.  Goodbye $*()[=/?!.  The only things I was left with were my
armor, vorpal blade, amulets of life saving and Yendor, ring of conflict,
a few wands of digging, and a lot of jewels.  Since I am an inveterate 
polypiler, I had long since eliminated spare rings, scrolls, potions, and 
such so it was going to be a long, long trip back up.

It looked like Bronwyn's life was going to be nasty, brutish, and short.

Potential spoilers ahead:























Whoever said that you need a lot of food in Gehennom was correct.  My
AoLS went when I starved about level 40.  With my 32 AC and at level 30
with over 250 HP, monsters weren't giving me much trouble, but they 
slowed me down.  When the Wizard showed up I nailed him with Finger of
Death until that got overused (and which didn't help my food problems). 
On the deserted mall level I picked up 12 tins of troll
meat and a tin opener (from a not very successful polypile) and that 
kept me going until I finally got out of Gehennom.

I grabbed every wand of teleportation that I saw (after the explosion I
had *no* overloading problems) and these proved useful later on, since
I couldn't ^Teleport with the AoY.

I used up my levitation spell casting ability on the water level (Jubilex?).
However, I had bridged the water on the Medusa level with a wand of cold 
earlier, so I got across that without any trouble.

Getting up the rest of the way was no particular problem.  The wizard 
appeared once or twice, but Vorpal Sword eventually got him.  I picked 
up some leftover rations from Fort Ludios so at least I didn't have to 
worry about starving.  But I still had only one ring (conflict) and one
amulet (Yendor).  No pickaxe (but a dozen or so wands of digging), no
way of levitating, no polymorph control.  I had also lost telepathy and
hadn't seen any floating eyes on the way out.  Ditching the amulet of
Aethiopea wasn't a good move, but I wasn't going to go back and look for 
it.

The elemental levels turned out to be relatively easy.  I had the most 
trouble on the earth level, mostly because I believed the talk about the
portal being far away from the entrance.  It wasn't.  By the time I had
killed the Wizard four or five times and used up ten or so wands of digging
I located it.  The fire level was a piece of cake, and would have been 
even faster if I had had a few more wands of light.  This time the portal
was near the center, and I wasted some time thoroughly exploring the far
side of the level.  Archons were a pain, but not a serious threat.
The air level was potentially a problem, since I had no reliable way of 
moving.  However, it was already lit, which made finding the portal
not very hard.  If the Wizard had shown up I would probably have been
dead meat.  The water level was easy, since there was nothing I could do
but stay in the bubble.  It took me right to the portal in about fifty
moves.

The final level lasted three moves.  Magic map (the most important spell
I learned), teleport to the temple of Thoth, and ascend.

What I need next time is an amulet of prevent stupidity.

Andrew Cockburn


